Add .axysymbols shared symbol state and -init flag

The old .axyvar file (a single number) is replaced by .axysymbols, which stores the complete symbol table after each successful compile: every variable with its allocated address, every constant, and every user label. The next compile in the same directory reads it back, so a variable can be assigned, compared and incremented from another .axy file, and constants or labels referenced by name. Variables declared without an axyvar now continue after the highest imported address.

- New -init flag resets the state file. Used alone it is silent; `axy -init -v` clears the file then shows the symbol report. Usage is now `axy [-v] [-init] input.axy [output.asm]`.
- Symbols are shared but defined only once — imported symbols are referenced, never re-emitted, so the .axy files are assembled together by BeebAsm.
- Duplicate or conflicting declarations are rejected with errors (`'score' already defined at &70`, `'base' already defined as '&70'`, `Label '.dup' already defined`). Re-declaring an identical definition is allowed, so recompiling a file is harmless.
- The -v symbol report now lists only the current file's own variables, constants and labels, and adds a Labels section.
- Fix: recompiling a file that declares constants no longer fails with "constant cannot be reassigned".
- README updated (usage, .axysymbols section, changelog).
